## 7. HIGH-VALUE AI-NATIVE FEATURES

Implement these as coherent, useful workflows rather than a collection of decorative AI buttons.

### FEATURE A — Patient Context Intelligence

Create a clinician-facing "What Matters Today" section.

Use existing patient information to highlight:

- Current reason for visit
- Relevant previous events
- Current medication and allergy context
- Recent clinical trends
- Missing clinical information
- Outstanding actions

Every clinical claim must link to its source when available.

Distinguish recorded fact, derived calculation, simulated AI suggestion, and unavailable data.

### FEATURE B — AI Pre-Visit Readiness

Create a visible readiness summary.

Examples:

- Demographics confirmed
- Medical history received
- Allergies reviewed
- External reports available
- Clinical intake incomplete
- Clinician review pending

Do not assign misleading readiness scores without documented criteria.

### FEATURE C — Intelligent Document-to-Chart

For synthetic demonstration documents:

- Extract relevant content.
- Show source snippets.
- Match information to supported structured EHR fields.
- Display confidence or uncertainty honestly.
- Require verification for ambiguous medical fields.
- Preserve original synthetic documents.
- Maintain source provenance.

If live Gemini extraction is not connected, use a clearly labeled deterministic synthetic extraction demonstration.

### FEATURE D — AI Clinical Action Composer

Convert information from reviewed consultation drafts into proposed downstream actions.

Examples:

- Draft laboratory order
- Draft medication request
- Draft follow-up task
- Draft patient instruction
- Draft referral request

Show proposal → review → approval → completed/failed status.

No autonomous clinician signature or actual prescription transmission.

### FEATURE E — Clinical Safety Radar

Use existing synthetic clinical information to display important safety checks, such as:

- Known allergy conflicts
- Critical test results
- Missing confirmation
- Incomplete reconciliation
- Overdue follow-up
- Unreviewed abnormal results

These are clinically meaningful prompts for review, not autonomous diagnoses.

### FEATURE F — Smart Role Handoff

When one authorized workflow step completes, make the next owner's work immediately visible.

Example:

Clinician approves lab order → Lab staff queue updates → Synthetic processing completes → Clinician results inbox updates → Care Coordinator receives authorized follow-up task.

Use real shared-state transitions.

### FEATURE G — Explainable AI Actions

Provide an unobtrusive "Why?" affordance on AI-assisted recommendations.

Show:

- Supporting synthetic record
- Relevant source
- Proposed operation
- Whether source information was verified
- Human decision required
- Final outcome

### FEATURE H — Patient Journey Time Machine

Create a visual, chronological journey replay.

The viewer can select a past synthetic event and see:

- Responsible role
- What happened
- Which record changed
- Why it advanced
- Which role received the next action

This should reuse the same audit/event model as the normal application.

### FEATURE I — Workflow Efficiency Evidence

Show actual measured interaction counts and avoid unsupported productivity claims.

Possible metrics:

- Manual user interactions performed
- Automated synthetic operations
- Role handoffs completed
- Repeated entries avoided
- Unresolved steps remaining

If comparing against a traditional workflow, clearly identify the baseline and measurement method.

Do not invent "70% faster" or similar savings figures.

## 8. A BETTER CLINICIAN UX

The application should remain a serious, professional clinical system.

The clinician should see two prominent workspaces.

### PRE-CONSULTATION

Purpose: Understand the patient before the consultation.

### POST-CONSULTATION

Purpose: Review, authorize and finalize the clinical work that occurred.

Detailed chart information must remain accessible from these summaries.

Avoid forcing clinicians to repeatedly open:

- History
- Medications
- Allergies
- Labs
- Orders
- Notes
- Tasks

when the relevant information can be meaningfully summarized in one view.

Keep clinical details available through source-linked drill-downs.

Do not remove necessary clinical documentation or safety controls simply to reduce clicks.

